# ComposeIO Notification Bridge [SPEC:CIO-NOTIFY]

> Delivers urgent Haven messages to David's iPhone — agents write to `urgent_messages` namespace, the bridge pushes to a physical device.

---

## 1. Problem

Haven agents sometimes need David's attention when he's away from a terminal — permission requests, governance votes requiring human approval, critical system alerts, or time-sensitive operational decisions. Currently there's no path from Haven to David's pocket. Messages sit in his inbox until he opens the Dashboard or starts a session.

---

## 2. Design Principles

### [DP-NOTIFY-1] Namespace-Driven, Not API-Driven

> Agents write to a namespace. The notification bridge subscribes. No agent needs to know how notifications are delivered — they just write to the right place.

- rationale: Decouples the "I need David's attention" intent from the delivery mechanism. Transport can change (Pushover today, SMS tomorrow) without touching any agent code or bootstrap.
- mechanism: Agents write to `haven::custodial_human::david/urgent_messages`. The bridge watches that namespace via MQTT.

### [DP-NOTIFY-2] Urgent Only — Not a Firehose

> This channel is for messages that need David's attention within hours, not minutes-old status updates. Abuse of urgency degrades the channel.

- rationale: Push notifications that arrive constantly get muted. The value of the channel is its scarcity.
- enforcement: Rate limiting at the bridge level. Agents should self-regulate — the bootstrap and agent definitions should clarify what constitutes "urgent."

### [DP-NOTIFY-3] Transport-Pluggable

> The notification transport is a plugin. The first implementation uses whichever transport David has available. Adding transports requires no changes to agents or namespaces.

- rationale: David may prefer Pushover today, HA companion app tomorrow, or ntfy.sh next week. The bridge adapts; nothing upstream changes.

---

## 3. Architecture

### [NOTIFY-ARCH] System Architecture

```
Agent writes to Haven:
  haven::custodial_human::david/urgent_messages
    ↓
Haven-ComposeIO Bridge publishes to MQTT:
  composeio/global/custodial_urgent
    ↓
Notification Bridge (Python, ComposeIO workload):
  - Subscribes to MQTT topic
  - Validates message (rate limit, dedup)
  - Dispatches via configured transport
    ↓
Transport Adapter:
  - Pushover API → iPhone push notification
  - OR: HA companion app → notify.mobile_app_*
  - OR: ntfy.sh → self-hosted push
  - OR: email → SMTP to David's email (push via Mail.app)
```

### [NOTIFY-FLOW] Data Flow

```
1. Agent writes urgent message to haven::custodial_human::david/urgent_messages
   Content: {title, body, priority, source_agent, context_url}
2. Haven-ComposeIO Bridge publishes to composeio/global/custodial_urgent
3. Notification Bridge receives MQTT message
4. Rate limiter checks: max 5 notifications per hour, max 20 per day
5. Dedup: same title+source_agent within 30 minutes → suppress
6. Transport adapter delivers push notification
7. Bridge logs delivery to bus storage for audit
```

---

## 4. Message Format

### [NOTIFY-MSG-FORMAT] Urgent Message Schema

Agents write to the `urgent_messages` namespace with this structure:

```yaml
title: "Permission Request: Deploy Haven v2.3 to production"
body: "Haven Builder requests approval to deploy the dashboard update. Changes include visibility canary and new namespace browser."
priority: high          # low, medium, high, critical
source_agent: haven-builder
context_url: null       # optional: link to discussion or dashboard view
category: permission    # permission, governance, alert, operational
```

### [NOTIFY-MSG-CATEGORIES] Message Categories

| Category | Description | Examples |
|----------|-------------|----------|
| `permission` | Agent needs David's approval to proceed | Deploy approval, namespace access request, external publication |
| `governance` | Constitutional matter requiring human input | Amendment vote, No-Confidence proceeding, bylaw adoption |
| `alert` | System health or integrity issue | Hash chain break, salt mismatch, service down |
| `operational` | Time-sensitive operational decision | Scheduled session conflict, resource limit approaching |

### [NOTIFY-PRIORITY] Priority Levels

| Priority | Push Behavior | Rate Limit | Use Case |
|----------|--------------|------------|----------|
| `low` | Silent push (no sound) | Standard (5/hr) | FYI that can wait |
| `medium` | Normal push | Standard (5/hr) | Needs attention today |
| `high` | Prominent push with sound | Standard (5/hr) | Needs attention within hours |
| `critical` | Persistent/repeated alert | Bypasses rate limit | System integrity at risk |

---

## 5. Transport Adapters

### [NOTIFY-TRANSPORT-INTERFACE] Adapter Contract

```python
class NotifyTransport:
    """Base interface for notification delivery transports."""

    async def send(self, title: str, body: str, priority: str, category: str) -> bool:
        """Deliver notification. Returns True on success."""
        ...

    def transport_id(self) -> str:
        """Unique identifier for this transport."""
        ...
```

### [NOTIFY-TRANSPORT-OPTIONS] Available Transports

| Transport | Setup | Cost | Pros | Cons |
|-----------|-------|------|------|------|
| **Pushover** | App install + API key | $5 one-time | Purpose-built, priority levels, quiet hours, rich formatting | Requires Pushover app |
| **HA Companion** | HA mobile app + notify service | Free (already have HA) | No new app, actionable notifications, integrates with HA automations | Requires HA companion app configured |
| **ntfy.sh** | Self-host or use public server | Free | Open source, self-hostable on mqtt-broker, simple curl API | Less rich than Pushover |
| **Email** | SMTP config | Free | Universal, no app install | Not truly "push", delivery delay |

- recommendation: Start with whichever David already has configured. If HA companion app is on his phone, use `notify.mobile_app_*` via HA REST API. If not, Pushover is the cleanest dedicated solution.
- multiple: The bridge can dispatch to multiple transports simultaneously (e.g., Pushover + email for redundancy).

### [NOTIFY-TRANSPORT-HA] Home Assistant Transport

```python
# Delivers via HA REST API → companion app push notification
async def send(self, title, body, priority, category):
    await httpx.post(
        f"{HA_URL}/api/services/notify/mobile_app_davids_iphone",
        headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {HA_TOKEN}"},
        json={
            "title": title,
            "message": body,
            "data": {
                "push": {"sound": "default" if priority != "low" else "none"},
                "group": category,
                "tag": f"haven-{category}",
            }
        }
    )
```

### [NOTIFY-TRANSPORT-PUSHOVER] Pushover Transport

```python
# Delivers via Pushover API → Pushover app push notification
PUSHOVER_PRIORITY = {"low": -1, "medium": 0, "high": 1, "critical": 2}

async def send(self, title, body, priority, category):
    await httpx.post(
        "https://api.pushover.net/1/messages.json",
        data={
            "token": PUSHOVER_APP_TOKEN,
            "user": PUSHOVER_USER_KEY,
            "title": f"[Haven] {title}",
            "message": body,
            "priority": PUSHOVER_PRIORITY[priority],
            "sound": "pushover" if priority in ("high", "critical") else "none",
        }
    )
```

---

## 6. Rate Limiting and Safety

### [NOTIFY-RATE-LIMIT] Rate Limits

| Window | Limit | Override |
|--------|-------|---------|
| Per hour | 5 notifications | `critical` priority bypasses |
| Per day | 20 notifications | `critical` priority bypasses |
| Dedup window | 30 minutes (same title + source) | None |

- rationale: Push notification fatigue is real. These limits protect the channel's value.
- critical-bypass: System integrity alerts (`critical` priority) bypass rate limits — if the hash chain breaks at 3am, David should know.
- overflow: Messages that exceed rate limits are queued and delivered in a digest at the next available window, tagged `[DIGEST: N queued]`.

### [NOTIFY-AUDIT] Delivery Audit

- every notification delivery (success or failure) logged to bus storage
- fields: timestamp, source_agent, category, priority, transport_used, delivery_status
- purpose: David can review notification history in the Dashboard; agents can check if their message was delivered

---

## 7. What Constitutes "Urgent"

### [NOTIFY-GUIDANCE] Agent Guidance for Urgency

This guidance should be incorporated into agent bootstraps and `/nudge` output:

| Situation | Urgent? | Category | Priority |
|-----------|---------|----------|----------|
| Need permission to deploy | Yes | permission | high |
| Amendment vote requires human approval | Yes | governance | high |
| Hash chain integrity failure | Yes | alert | critical |
| Service down on mqtt-broker | Yes | alert | high |
| Builder milestone completed | **No** — use dev channel | — | — |
| Routine governance notification | **No** — use legal-notices | — | — |
| Interesting insight to share | **No** — use shared tier | — | — |
| Session ended normally | **No** — logged automatically | — | — |

- principle: If David would want to be woken up for it, it's `critical`. If he'd want to see it before his next session, it's `high`. If it can wait for the Dashboard, it's not urgent — don't push it.

---

## 8. Deployment

### [NOTIFY-DEPLOY] Service Deployment

- host: mqtt-broker.local
- runtime: Python 3.12, Docker container or standalone process
- dependencies: MQTT broker at :1884, HA REST API (if using HA transport), or Pushover API key
- config: `/var/lib/composeio/notification-bridge/config.yaml`

```yaml
mqtt:
  host: mqtt-broker.local
  port: 1884
  topic: composeio/global/custodial_urgent
  qos: 1

transport:
  primary: pushover          # or: ha_companion, ntfy, email
  pushover:
    app_token: ${PUSHOVER_APP_TOKEN}
    user_key: ${PUSHOVER_USER_KEY}
  ha_companion:
    ha_url: http://homeassistant.local:8123
    ha_token: ${HA_TOKEN}
    service: notify.mobile_app_davids_iphone

rate_limit:
  per_hour: 5
  per_day: 20
  dedup_window_minutes: 30
  critical_bypasses_limits: true
```

---

## 9. Testing

### [NOTIFY-TEST-INTEGRATION] Integration Tests

| Test | Method | Expected Result |
|------|--------|----------------|
| MQTT receipt | Publish test message to `composeio/global/custodial_urgent` | Bridge receives and logs |
| Transport delivery | Send test notification via configured transport | Push notification arrives on device |
| Rate limiting | Send 6 messages in 1 minute | 5 delivered, 1 queued |
| Dedup | Send identical message twice within 30 minutes | Second suppressed |
| Critical bypass | Send `critical` message when rate limit exhausted | Delivered despite limit |
| Digest | Exceed rate limit with 3 queued messages | Digest delivered at next window |
| Audit logging | Send message, check bus storage | Delivery record present |

### [NOTIFY-TEST-E2E] End-to-End Test

```
1. Agent writes to haven::custodial_human::david/urgent_messages:
   {title: "Test", body: "Notification bridge test", priority: "low",
    source_agent: "haven-architect", category: "operational"}

2. Haven-ComposeIO Bridge publishes to composeio/global/custodial_urgent

3. Notification Bridge receives, validates, dispatches via configured transport

4. Verify: push notification arrives on David's iPhone (silent, low priority)

5. Verify: delivery logged in bus storage audit
```

---

## Invariants

### [INV-NOTIFY-1] Namespace Is the Only Interface

- rule: Agents interact with the notification system exclusively by writing to `haven::custodial_human::david/urgent_messages`. No agent should call push APIs directly.
- enforcement: Transport credentials are in the bridge config only — agents have no access to Pushover tokens or HA tokens.

### [INV-NOTIFY-2] Rate Limits Non-Negotiable (Except Critical)

- rule: Rate limits are enforced at the bridge level. No agent can override them. Only `critical` priority (system integrity) bypasses limits.
- enforcement: Bridge-side rate limiter, not agent-side honor system.

### [INV-NOTIFY-3] Delivery Audited

- rule: Every notification attempt (success or failure) is logged to bus storage with full context.
- enforcement: Logging happens before and after transport dispatch.

